#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193826 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to compute inverses of reciprocal functions.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the function $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{7}{2y+4}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{7}{2y+4} \\\\ x(2y+4) & =7 \\\\ 2xy+4x & =7 \\\\ 2xy & =7-4x \\\\ y & =\\frac{7-4x}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{7 - 4x}{2x}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1be3fc12-b770-5a76-ab56-1385a320d85f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6512 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
